+## Platform surface rule
+
+Both iOS and web maintain the same disciplined surface architecture:
+- **No glassmorphism, no custom materials, and no backdrop blur on any platform.**
+- All cards, sheets, containers, and navigation chrome are strictly **opaque** Obsidian (`#0A0B10`) or Slate (`#171A22`).
+- Separation and hierarchy come purely from opaque tonal surface contrast (Obsidian field vs. Slate elevated surfaces), restrained 1 pt Chalk hairline borders (`stroke/subtle`, `stroke/default`), and the approved selective transparent-to-Obsidian photo fade.
+- Native OS system presentations are standard platform chrome, not custom glass cards.
 ## Semantic color rules
 
-- Green is not a substitute for Chalk body text.
-- A sent attempt uses green plus a checkmark and the word “Sent.”
-- A failed attempt uses red plus a cross and an explicit outcome.
-- A selected route uses green line/node treatment plus a selected state.
-- Destructive system actions never use green.
-- A climbing fall is an activity outcome—not an app error.
-- System failures and climbing outcomes must have separate visual semantics.
+- **Indigo is agency:** Primary buttons, active tabs, focus states, and interactive filter chips use Indigo (`#345CFF`).
+- **Green is send status:** Successful climbs, sent checkmarks, and PR highlights use Send Green (`#32D583`).
+- **Send kudos is neutral:** The kudos action uses a rounded pill, a neutral outlined heart, and Chalk text (`Send kudos · 34`). It is **never** colored green.
+- **Outcome distinction:** Sent uses `#32D583` with a checkmark and "Sent". Fell uses `#FF6B64` with a cross and "Fell". Stopped uses `#FF6B64` with an explicit icon and "Stopped".
+- **Falls are climbing outcomes:** A climbing fall is recorded as an athletic outcome, never as an application system error.
 
